1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:01,801
Hi, I'm Sohan.

2
00:00:01,801 --> 00:00:03,881
This is the same inspection app,

3
00:00:03,881 --> 00:00:07,481
rebuilt with two React code components inside Canvas.

4
00:00:07,738 --> 00:00:09,858
It takes the places where Canvas stopped,

5
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:12,968
and shows what it costs to cross that line.

6
00:00:13,231 --> 00:00:15,631
The Canvas build hit real limits.

7
00:00:15,631 --> 00:00:17,711
The checklist stopped at level five,

8
00:00:17,711 --> 00:00:20,271
and a safety question never appeared.

9
00:00:20,528 --> 00:00:24,708
Rules were hard-coded, and photo markup was freehand only,

10
00:00:24,708 --> 00:00:26,768
saved apart from the photo.

11
00:00:27,782 --> 00:00:30,762
The screens around the components are still standard Canvas,

12
00:00:30,762 --> 00:00:33,362
on the same Dataverse tables.

13
00:00:34,984 --> 00:00:36,924
An inspection starts the same way,

14
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:39,764
with a site and a piece of equipment.

15
00:00:43,528 --> 00:00:46,188
Now the checklist is one code component,

16
00:00:46,188 --> 00:00:48,488
written in React and TypeScript.

17
00:00:48,744 --> 00:00:52,364
This forklift is out of service, with damage in the hydraulics,

18
00:00:52,364 --> 00:00:54,484
and fluid is leaking.

19
00:00:54,742 --> 00:00:57,002
No spill kit has been deployed.

20
00:00:57,002 --> 00:00:59,202
A compound rule adds fifteen points,

21
00:00:59,202 --> 00:01:02,632
and it's a row in Dataverse, not a formula.

22
00:01:02,886 --> 00:01:04,436
And here's the sixth level,

23
00:01:04,436 --> 00:01:07,256
the question Canvas could never show.

24
00:01:07,515 --> 00:01:09,035
It's a critical stop,

25
00:01:09,035 --> 00:01:11,685
and the score reaches the cap of one hundred.

26
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,720
Code components in canvas apps cannot call Dataverse,

27
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:18,750
so the app passes the data in as JSON.

28
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,100
The component hands the answers,

29
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:24,870
the score and the explanation back through output properties.

30
00:01:28,651 --> 00:01:31,011
The second component is the photo annotator,

31
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:35,781
with numbered markers, arrows, boxes and freehand.

32
00:01:36,039 --> 00:01:40,039
Every mark saves as data, and burned into a copy of the photo,

33
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:42,979
so the original stays clean.

34
00:01:46,732 --> 00:01:48,572
Review reads the component's outputs,

35
00:01:48,572 --> 00:01:51,832
and Submit saves everything to Dataverse.

36
00:01:56,468 --> 00:02:00,128
The new inspection goes straight to the top of the list.

37
00:02:00,405 --> 00:02:01,485
Under the hood,

38
00:02:01,485 --> 00:02:05,055
that's two React components and seven Dataverse tables.

39
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:08,410
Twenty-three Playwright tests drive the published app,

40
00:02:08,410 --> 00:02:12,120
and fifteen unit tests cover the components' logic.

41
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:16,139
All of this is sample data, in a developer environment.

42
00:02:16,422 --> 00:02:18,822
Canvas still does most of this app.

43
00:02:18,822 --> 00:02:21,802
The code components took only the parts it couldn't.

44
00:02:22,059 --> 00:02:24,979
The full build, the tests and the scripts are on my GitHub,

45
00:02:24,979 --> 00:02:28,769
and there's more on my portfolio. Thanks for watching.
